Whether an 8-bit quantized detector stays reliable under corruption cannot be read off the format label: it depends on how the engine was built and which model region is quantized. We pair every corrupted input with a codec-matched clean control, so identical bytes reach executable TensorRT INT8 and FP8 engines, and estimate the corruption-associated change in the FP8--INT8 average-precision (AP) gap, $\Delta E$, inside common-image bootstrap samples. Across nine dataset$\times$scale blocks of YOLO11 on KITTI, VOC, and a 2,000-image COCO subset, $\Delta E$ is strongly heterogeneous ($I^2=\nnHetIsq\%$) and both signs survive Holm correction, even though FP8 exceeds INT8 on absolute clean and corrupted AP in every matched block mean. In RetinaNet, a matched-recipe decomposition localizes a severe INT8 level deficit ($-$\nnMatchedCleanDeficitKitti{} AP on KITTI) to the regression head: leaving only that head in floating point restores clean-competitive accuracy without retraining ($\nnArmSelCleanKitti$ vs.\ $\nnArmFeCleanKitti$ AP for matched FP8 on original clean inputs) and matches FP8 on the KITTI corrupt mean, leaving a residual that widens under corruption to about one AP point on VOC. A two-fold intervention, each fold frozen before its own runs, then calibrates INT8 on corrupted images and tests unseen families. The frozen held-out criterion---read directionally, so a significant harm counts as failure---is met in two of four fold$\times$dataset cells, with a significant across-the-board harm in a third; a corruption-specific held-out benefit is significantly positive in exactly one cell, on both intervention tiers. Codec-matched calibration controls (post hoc for fold~1, pre-specified for fold~2) bound the codec level contribution far below the INT8 level deficit, though codec interaction effects are the same order as the corruption-specific components. A post-hoc operand-level factorial inside the head attributes essentially all of the deficit to activation quantization---INT8 head weights contribute at most a $\sim$0.2-AP residual---with an operand interaction compatible with zero, while the same protocol on anchor-free FCOS shows no head-specific deficit---head exclusion changes nothing measurable---showing the fragility is not a generic property of INT8 detectors. Three conclusions follow: regression-head exclusion (or, in the tested recipe, promoting only the head's activations) repairs the observed level deficit in the tested recipe without retraining (deployment cost not evaluated, and its strongest check is development-contaminated); corruption-aware calibration is inconsistent for held-out transfer; and the mechanism is activation-side but the operative failure mode---range clipping versus bin resolution---remains untested. Quantization robustness should be reported per recipe and per model region.
